{In chess, a skewer is an attack upon two pieces in a line and is similar to a
pin. A skewer is sometimes described as a "reverse pin"; the difference is
that in a skewer, the more valuable piece is in front of the piece of lesser
value. The opponent is compelled to move the more valuable piece to avoid its
capture, thereby exposing the less valuable piece which can then be captured.
The current position is a typical skewer:} 1. Qb5+ {White gives a check and
forces the king to go away, leaving the queen falling "for free".} Ke6 2. Qxg5
$18 *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Skewer"]
[Black "sample 2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "R7/P4k2/8/8/8/8/7K/r7 w - - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "5"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.29"]

{If it was Black's turn, we would reach a theoretical drawn endgame after Kg7.
However the current misplacement of the black king allows White to win the
game by using the skewer motif!} 1. Rh8 {White intends to promote his pawn
which would mean the end of the game and after} Rxa7 2. Rh7+ {The skewer!} Ke6
3. Rxa7 $18 {Try to obtain decisive material advantage in each position by
using the help of skewer!} *

